 last time i was on a  set that actually looks quite like this  was the morning show set and that was in  april um it is it's weird except  honestly  i don't i i couldn't do what you do  it's early every day we couldn't do what  you do  about it at least you don't have to  memorize your lines right you get to  read them you said recently that you've  been stopped on the street uh by people  who love the morning show why do you  think they're connecting to this  character in particular it's so bizarre  you know i did the good wife for seven  years  22 episodes a year wow and i would walk  in and out of places and people would  only recognize me if they heard my voice  and then they'd turn around and say oh  you're the but now i walk my dog at  night with a mask and a baseball hat on  and i have people saying i love you on  the morning show and i'm like  wow i guess there's something about  maybe it is you'd have to ask them i  don't know but i think there's something  about laura where  the way they wrote her honestly i think  anyone whoever had played this role i  got lucky enough to do it but they wrote  her so  openly and honestly it's refreshing and  it's sort of i think something at least  i aspire to be more like her in in just  living your truth right so maybe that's  what it is i don't know  you've talked about preparing for roles  and wanting to know the history  who did you speak to or what guidance  did you get to prepare for this news  anchor woman who's accomplished so much  and please include a cbs person  yes that would be nice that'd be a nice  plug but who was your inspiration who  did you well you know first of all i got  the role in the middle of the lockdown  so it was november of of 2020  and um there weren't a lot of places i  could go or  or research i could do outside of my own  home  but i happen to i know this is going to  sound um i'm not meaning to name drop at  all but i happen to know diane sawyer a  little bit i know katie couric i know  george stephanopoulos through the years  gail king is a good friend of mine and  she's not here today so i i have spirit  i have been able to  to sort of get a taste of of what  journalists go through and what they do  and i tried to  emulate uh laura on sort of a a a mix of  all of them because she she is uba's  star of night she has her nighttime show  but she also is a woman who  did field work and she's also a woman  who got fired for being gay so she had  to start from scratch you know she was  on a morning show got fired and started  from scratch and came back because she  was a great journalist not because it  not because it had anything to do with  her sexuality now speaking of her  sexuality you said you didn't hesitate  to play a gay character not a spark  conversation in the lgbtq plus community  um saying well actors and actresses in  that community can better portray a  character like her what is your response  to that  well um i i can understand that um my  response also would be we're all making  assumptions as to who i am  and what my past is and what all of our  paths are i understand 100  that i can't play a different race  right but i am an actress  and i am supposed to embody  another character  whatever their sexuality is doesn't  matter to me the same way  uh watching a gay person play a straight  person are you telling me that because  i'm a mother i can never play a woman  who's never had a child  or  you know if you've never been married  that you can't play a married woman i  mean you you have to be careful  yeah on on where you're drawing the line  there we're actors right we're supposed  to embody a character  regardless of their sexuality when it  comes to  race  uh and gender that's a whole different  story right and and i 100 agree with  that right so that's that's  that's my stance on it well well said  and i look forward to watching the rest  of the season of the morning show thank  you brianna margelise thank you so much  for joining us

